window.ATLAS_CARDS = {"capability-accessibility-model":{"name":"The Capability-Accessibility Model","steps":[{"label":"The Access Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">The Capability-Accessibility Model</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">If you know how to do something, can you always do it?</p>"},{"ask":"You know your best friend’s phone number, or a line from a song you have sung a hundred times. Now picture standing up to give a toast, and your mind going blank on it. Five minutes later, sitting down, it comes flooding back. When your mind went “blank,” what actually happened to what you were going to say?","choices":["For those five minutes it was gone","It was there the whole time, and something stood between you and it","It depends how nervous you were"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>It was there the whole time. Nothing about your memory changed in five minutes. What changed was your brain state, and the state you were in could not reach the thing you own completely.</p><p>Which means that what you are capable of at any given moment is determined by two separate things: the knowledge and skills you’ve built through learning, and the knowledge and skills you can actually access at that moment.</p>"},{"chunk":"<p>There’s a patient in the book The Genius and the Impostor named Dennis who suffered a stroke. As a result, Dennis could still write a sentence but couldn’t read the sentence he just wrote. The stroke had cut the connections that carried what his eyes saw over to the part of his brain that reads.</p><p class=\"lf-cite\">Alexia without agraphia, a disconnection syndrome</p>"},{"ask":"So what does Dennis still have, and what has he lost?","choices":["Dennis has lost the ability to read. The stroke destroyed it","Dennis still has the ability to read. What he has lost is the connection between his eyes and the reading machinery","Dennis has lost both the ability to read and the connection to it"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Dennis still has all of the neural machinery needed to read. But because of the stroke, visual information can’t get into that machinery. This is similar to what happens when your mind “goes blank” - what you need still exists in your brain, you just can’t get to it.</p>"},{"ask":"Researchers taught people a number puzzle with a shortcut hidden in it that nobody was told about. Then one group slept, while two other groups stayed awake. The sleepers were more than twice as likely to come back and spot the shortcut. All three groups had the same training, and nobody was taught anything while they were asleep. So what did sleep change?","choices":["Sleep added something the awake groups never got. The sleepers knew more when they came back","Sleep didn't change what anybody knew. It changed what they could get to","Sleep left them more alert, so they tried harder at the puzzle"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Sleep produced an entirely different pattern of brain activity. That pattern allowed the brain to arrive at a solution that the waking brain could not.</p><p class=\"lf-cite\">Wagner et al., 2004 (sleep and insight)</p>"},{"ask":"We assume that the knowledge and skills we’ve acquired are always accessible. And that what we’re capable of doesn’t change over the course of the day. And that if we had an ability, we’d know about it. But given what we’ve reviewed so far, does that hold up?","choices":["Yes, the belief holds. Real skill shows up when it matters","No. Having a skill and being able to reach that skill in the moment are two different things","The belief holds for physical skills but not for mental ones"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>What you are capable of at any moment depends on two things. First, what you’ve learned up until that point. And second, what your brain can access.</p><p>That also means some of what you’ve learned can stay hidden, if you’re never in a brain state that lets it surface. Most of the people who stayed awake in the sleep study had everything they needed to find the shortcut, and they didn’t find it. If that test had been their only try, nobody would have known they could, including them.</p>"},{"chunk":"<p>Think of a smartphone loaded up with apps. Navigation, photo editing, translation, music streaming. Each one can do whatever its code lets it do.</p><p>But having the app on your phone isn’t enough. For the app to be useful, you must be able to get information into it.</p><p>A Spanish translation app can't help you find a bathroom in Acapulco if you can't open it.</p><p>The same is true with your brain. Only your brain wrote the code for every one of its apps. That’s the learning process.</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">What you are capable of at any given moment is determined by two separate things: the knowledge and skills you’ve built through learning, and the knowledge and skills you can actually access at that moment.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">Capability and accessibility are central ideas in The Genius and the Impostor. Dennis, from this station, is one of the patients in the book.</p>"}],"next":{"id":"routing-architecture","name":"Routing Architecture","reason":"What decides which of your skills you can reach at a given moment?"},"links":[{"id":"solution-space","name":"Solution Space"},{"id":"the-impostor","name":"The Impostor"}],"game":null},"automaticity":{"name":"Automaticity","steps":[{"label":"The Machinery Line · The Genius Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">Automaticity</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">What happens in your brain when you get good at something?</p>"},{"ask":"Think back to the first time you drove a car. Could you have held a real conversation while safely navigating through traffic?","choices":["Yes, you could easily have held a conversation while driving.","No. Driving took all of your attention and there was none left for a conversation","You could have held a conversation only while parallel parking."],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Of course not. Every scrap of your attention was focused on the task of driving, with nothing left over for conversations about dinner.</p><p>And today you can probably talk and drive without a problem. So what changed?</p>"},{"ask":"What changed between when you first started driving and now?","choices":["You became less cautious","Driving moved to the part of your brain that runs without you feeling it","You got better at paying attention to two things at once"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>The driving moved to the part of your brain that runs without you feeling it.</p><p>And this is what happens when we learn anything. Research shows that we get better at a task by building dedicated circuits in the brain that don’t require conscious control.</p>"},{"chunk":"<p>Our conscious mind can only pay attention to one thing at a time, and can hold only about four items in memory. Building task-specific circuits through learning is how we escape this limitation, referred to in the book as the conscious bottleneck.</p><p class=\"lf-cite\">Working memory limits (Cowan, 2001)</p>"},{"ask":"Reading a sentence needs letter shapes, sounds, spellings, meanings and grammar, all at the same time. If reading had to run through our conscious mind, what would reading be like?","choices":["Reading would be about the same, just a little slower","Reading would be slow and effortful, one letter at a time, the way a six-year-old reads","Reading would be no different, because the channel is fast enough"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>We’d never get past the slow and effortful stage of reading one letter at a time, like we did when we first started. And so we’d never be able to understand any of what we read.</p><p>As you read this right now, however, your understanding is effortless and immediate. All you have to do is direct your eyes to these words, and your brain delivers meaning into your conscious mind. This is because, when you learned to read, your brain built all the complex circuitry required to perform the dozens of tasks that now make this possible.</p><p>Your conscious mind doesn’t do any of the work of reading. You use it to point your attention at things, so that the non-conscious skill networks you’ve built (in this case the ones for reading) can be called into action.</p>"},{"ask":"We tend to think that the things we can do “automatically,” without exerting conscious effort, must be simpler forms of cognition. It feels to us that the real “thinking” is the slow, effortful kind you can feel yourself doing. But does that hold up with the driving and reading examples we’ve discussed?","choices":["Driving and reading are mindless routines, not real thinking","Driving and reading are the mind doing its most complex work","Driving and reading are somewhere in between routine and thinking"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>They're your mind doing its most complex work.</p><p>The effortful cognition we tend to label as “thinking” is instead what happens when our brain encounters a problem it doesn’t know how to solve. Because of the limitations of the conscious mind, the only way we can eventually solve it is by learning, so that we can move the “thinking” to the powerful, non-conscious networks that we don’t feel working.</p><p>This ability to build specialized problem solving networks is the only way we can build our intelligence.</p>"},{"ask":"Learning curves have the same shape whether you are training face recognition, a tennis serve, or algebra: a fast rise, then a leveling off. What do you think that similarity suggests about what’s happening in the brain?","choices":["The shared shape of the learning curve is a coincidence","The shared shape of the learning curve points to one mechanism underneath all skills","The shared shape of the learning curve means all skills are equally hard"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>It points to a single mechanism that powers all of them.</p><p>Whatever a skill looks like from the outside, it gets built the same way. Practice moves the work off the channel you can feel and onto machinery that runs on its own.</p><p>Whether it’s for a perceptual skill like face recognition, a motor skill like tennis, or a mental skill like math, the brain builds expertise in the same way.</p><p class=\"lf-cite\">Power-law learning across domains (Newell &amp; Rosenbloom, 1981)</p>"},{"ask":"A bat finds its way in the dark by echolocation. It was born with this ability wired into its brain, and it runs automatically. Your ability to read automatically is wired into your brain, and you built that wiring through learning. Which of these is a sign of intelligence - navigating by echolocation, reading, both, or neither?","choices":["The bat's echolocation is instinct, so it does not count as intelligence. Your reading does","Your reading is just an automatic habit now. The bat's echolocation is the real intelligence","Both are examples of problem solving, and thus examples of high level intelligence."],"correct":2,"answer":"<p>Both are examples of high level intelligence. How the wiring got there doesn’t change that, nor does the fact that they can be performed fast and “automatically.”</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">Practice something enough times and you can do without “thinking.” It runs on its own, fast, in the background, while your attention is elsewhere. This process isn’t a shortcut around thinking, but the only way real thinking can ever happen.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">The book calls automaticity the engine of intelligence and devotes a full chapter to it.</p>"}],"next":{"id":"effort-inversion","name":"Effort Inversion","reason":"Why do we mistake effortless skill for simple skill?"},"links":[{"id":"automaticity-blindness","name":"Automaticity Blindness"},{"id":"progressive-composition","name":"Progressive Composition"},{"id":"the-genius","name":"The Genius"}],"game":{"name":"The Unstoppable Reader","url":"../unstoppable-reader.html"}},"effort-inversion":{"name":"Effort Inversion","steps":[{"label":"The Bias Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">Effort Inversion</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">When something feels effortless to you, what does that tell you about how hard it is?</p>"},{"ask":"Picture two jugglers. One keeps eight balls in the air without a miss. The other is struggling just to juggle two, dropping them frequently. Which one is doing the harder thing?","choices":["The sweating juggler is doing the harder thing, which you can see from her effort.","The juggler with eight balls is doing the harder thing","You cannot tell which juggler is doing the harder thing from the outside"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>The one with eight balls is obviously performing the harder task, by a long shot. And nobody would claim otherwise.</p><p>Remember how obvious this answer felt.</p>"},{"ask":"An experienced doctor is consulted to see a patient and within 10 minutes has arrived at the correct diagnosis and course of treatment. A medical student examines the same patient for an hour. He thinks through the case for another 30 minutes, generates three possible diagnoses, and orders multiple tests to try to determine which it is. Upon completion of the tests, a correct diagnosis is made. Who displayed signs of greater intelligence - the experienced doctor or the medical student?","choices":["The student showed more intelligence because he did much more “thinking”","The experienced doctor is showing more intelligence because he solved the problem much more efficiently and effectively"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>The experienced doctor. Intelligence is demonstrated by efficient and effective problem solving. Both brains were thinking through the case, but in one instance the thinking was performed by non-conscious, expert networks, and in the other instance it was performed by conscious, novice networks.</p>"},{"ask":"A locksmith opens your lock in thirty seconds and charges you sixty dollars. Another one sweats and fumbles for an hour and charges the same. Which one do most people feel got the fair deal?","choices":["The fast locksmith gave the fair deal. Thirty seconds of skill for sixty dollars is a bargain","The slow locksmith gave the fair deal. At least you watched him work for the money","Both locksmiths gave the same deal, because the price was the same"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Most people think the slow locksmith gave the better deal, and the fast one was a rip-off.</p><p>In fact, locksmiths report that their tips are better when it takes them longer.</p><p>When we can see expertise with our own eyes, as with the juggler, we can appreciate that making a complex task look easy is an illusion.</p><p>When we don’t see it, we assume that making something look easy means that it actually was.</p>"},{"ask":"What this reveals is that if a problem looks effortful to solve, we assume it must be a complex one. And if it looks easy, we assume it must be an easy one. Knowing what you now know, what’s the reality?","choices":["The belief is right. Effort is an accurate indicator of difficulty","The belief is backwards. Ease is the sign of mastery, and visible struggle is what a beginner looks like","Whether effort signals difficulty depends on the job"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Our intuitions here are wrong. Effortlessness is a sign of mastery, not simplicity.</p><p>Easy doesn't mean it’s simple, it means it can run without conscious control. We only get this right when the skill is one we can actually watch, like juggling, and we get it wrong just about everywhere else.</p><p>Michelangelo said it best: “If people knew how hard I worked to get my mastery, it wouldn’t seem so wonderful at all.”</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">We watch somebody struggle and decide the task must be hard. We watch somebody breeze through it and decide it must be easy. But the reverse is almost always what’s actually true. Struggle is what beginning looks like and ease is what mastery looks like.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">The book covers this in a section titled “Easy ≠ Simple.”</p>"}],"next":{"id":"competence-inversion","name":"Competence Inversion","reason":"How does the same mistake shape the way we judge experts?"},"links":[{"id":"automaticity","name":"Automaticity"},{"id":"automaticity-blindness","name":"Automaticity Blindness"}],"game":{"name":"The Unstoppable Reader","url":"../unstoppable-reader.html"}},"automaticity-blindness":{"name":"Automaticity Blindness","steps":[{"label":"The Bias Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">Automaticity Blindness</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">How much of your brain’s work can you actually feel?</p>"},{"ask":"Notice what it feels like as you read this sentence. You look at the words, and the meaning comes. In order for that to happen, hundreds of small tasks must be completed simultaneously: recognizing each letter and word, associating each word and phrase with its meaning, applying the rules of grammar and syntax, holding the words in memory until the sentence ends. And yet, you don’t feel any of that work being done. Why not?","choices":["Because reading is easy","Because those tasks are being handled by expert systems that run beneath conscious awareness","Because you were born with this ability."],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>You don’t feel it because those tasks are being handled by expert systems that run beneath conscious awareness. You built those systems when you learned how to read.</p>"},{"ask":"You spot a friend's face in a crowd of hundreds, instantly. Could you say how you did it? What you compared, what you ruled out?","choices":["Yes, you could roughly describe how you picked out the face","No. The face showed up with no sense of any work being done","You could describe it only if you had seen the friend recently"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Not a chance. Pulling one face out of hundreds is some of the hardest computation your brain does, and you experience exactly none of it. The answer just arrives, with no sense of the work behind it.</p>"},{"ask":"So here is the question this station is about. Why can you feel yourself struggling to remember a new ten digit phone number, but not feel yourself reading or recognizing a face?","choices":["Because remembering ten digits is harder than reading or recognizing a face","Because you only feel your mind working when it requires your conscious attention. Reading and face recognition run in non-conscious, expert networks you don’t feel","Because reading and recognizing faces are habits, not thinking"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Because you only feel your mind working on the things you aren’t expert at.</p><p>Remembering ten digits is a trivially simple task compared to reading or recognizing a face. But you have to use your unskilled, single-tasking conscious mind to do it, and your conscious mind can only hold about 4 things in memory.</p><p>Learning to read and recognize a face once felt hard, too, until you developed the machinery that made you an expert at them. Now they run through the part of your brain you don’t feel.</p><p>So the better you get at something, the less of it you feel. That's automaticity blindness.</p>"},{"ask":"Walking across a cluttered room feels like nothing. Yet it requires exquisite balance and the rapid coordination of dozens of muscles. Which is why our best robots still fall over doing it. So what does the feeling of nothing tell you about the work required to pull it off?","choices":["The feeling of nothing tells you there is not much work in walking","The feeling of nothing tells you nothing about the work. It is what machinery feels like when we’ve gotten really good","The feeling of nothing tells you walking is simpler than it looks"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>It tells you nothing about the work. The feeling of nothing is what machinery feels like when you’ve gotten really good.</p><p>Because of this, we are predisposed to overlooking and dismissing the most extraordinary feats that our minds perform.</p>"},{"chunk":"<p>You've already experienced this process from start to finish with reading. You remember sounding out c-a-t, slowly, every bit of it effortful and conscious. Now you can't look at a word and not read it.</p><p>The whole process dropped out of your awareness the moment you got good. That's what learning does to every motor, perceptual, or mental skill you've ever built.</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">Once a skill runs on its own, you can't feel it working. You only notice the effortful parts of your own mind, so the automatic parts go silent and start to feel like nothing at all, right at the moment they're doing the best and most advanced work you do.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">The book explains how this blindness leads to a whole set of mistaken beliefs about intelligence.</p>"}],"next":{"id":"everyday-genius","name":"Everyday Genius","reason":"How complex are the skills you use every day?"},"links":[{"id":"effort-inversion","name":"Effort Inversion"},{"id":"introspective-bias","name":"Introspective Bias"}],"game":null},"introspective-bias":{"name":"Introspective Bias","steps":[{"label":"The Bias Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">Introspective Bias</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">How would you figure out how your own mind works?</p>"},{"ask":"Imagine writing a complete theory of how digestion works without ever studying the stomach or the intestines. Or a theory of blood circulation without ever studying the heart or blood vessels. What would you call those theories?","choices":["Those theories would likely be incomplete but useful","Those theories are almost guaranteed to be wrong.","As long as they feel right, those theories are likely to be right."],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Theories of digestion and circulation that are written without studying the organs that perform those functions are virtually guaranteed to be wrong.</p><p>And yet that describes the first theories of human intelligence - the ones that still influence how most people think about it today. A hundred years of serious work on what makes people smart, and you could become a world expert in the field without ever once thinking about a neuron. We built an entire model of a biological function without regard to the organ that produces it.</p>"},{"ask":"So where did that model of human intelligence come from, if not the brain? What was the evidence the people building it actually had?","choices":["The model of intelligence came from brain scans, from the start","The model of intelligence came from what thinking feels like, plus tests based on those feelings","The model of intelligence came from animal studies"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>It came from what thinking feels like to us.</p><p>Slow, deliberate, effortful problem solving is the part of our mind we can feel. So we assumed that was the part doing the best work, and the part that made us special. Everything else got left out of the definition.</p>"},{"ask":"You already know something about that inside view from the last few stations. The most complex problems our brain solves, like reading, recognizing a face, or walking a cluttered room, feel effortless. So if you build a theory of the mind from what you can feel, what do you leave out?","choices":["A theory built from what you can feel leaves out a few edge cases","A theory built from what you can feel leaves out almost everything the mind does, including all of its most extraordinary feats","A theory built from what you can feel leaves out only the physical skills"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>You leave out almost the entire mind, including all of its most extraordinary feats.</p><p>The part you can feel is the small, slow, effortful part. The rest, which is very nearly everything, happens where introspection can't reach. So the theory mistook the one tiny lit corner for the whole house.</p>"},{"ask":"Now consider the kinds of problems that are used on an IQ test, which was first created over a century ago. Puzzles, sequences, remembering digits. They are chosen because they feel hard. What does that tell us about how it’s defining “intelligence”?","choices":["Puzzles and digit spans are used because they’re the hardest tasks","IQ tests measure the part of the mind that people could feel","IQ tests measure everything the mind does"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>IQ tests measure the kind of thinking you can feel yourself doing, because the part that felt effortless seemed ordinary and insignificant. Any task that most people could perform with minimal conscious effort was excluded.</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">We built our whole picture of thinking out of the one part of our mind that we feel, which is slow conscious deliberation. Everything the mind does automatically is invisible from the inside, so we ignored it altogether.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">The book argues that this bias underlies most of our mistaken beliefs about intelligence.</p>"}],"next":{"id":"trained-reasoning","name":"Trained Reasoning","reason":"Is reasoning really the general ability it feels like?"},"links":[{"id":"effort-inversion","name":"Effort Inversion"},{"id":"competence-inversion","name":"Competence Inversion"},{"id":"special-brain-story","name":"The Special Brain Story"}],"game":null},"competence-inversion":{"name":"Competence Inversion","steps":[{"label":"The Bias Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">Competence Inversion</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">How can you tell when someone is thinking hard?</p>"},{"ask":"A chess amateur sweats over the board, calculating line after line. A grandmaster glances at the same position and moves, feeling nothing. By the way we usually judge thinking, which one looks like they are doing the real thinking?","choices":["The amateur looks like the one doing the real thinking","The grandmaster looks like the one doing the real thinking","Neither looks like they are thinking, because chess is not thinking"],"correct":0,"answer":"<p>By how we commonly conceive of it, the amateur is doing more “thinking.” All that visible calculating looks like the real thing, and the grandmaster's glance looks like instinct.</p><p>But the grandmaster wins.</p>"},{"ask":"Early in a doctor's training, a diagnosis takes an hour of effortful examination. Years later the same doctor has it in the first minute, and is far more often right. Which version was the rigorous one?","choices":["The hour-long diagnosis was the rigorous one. That was careful thinking","The one-minute diagnosis was the rigorous one. The hour-long version was the less competent version","Both diagnoses were rigorous, in different ways"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>The one-minute version.</p><p>That effortful hour wasn't careful thinking at its finest. It was the same doctor's skill before it had finished being built. An expert’s brain can work through a problem far faster and more effectively, because the work is being done in fast, multi-tasking expert networks.</p>"},{"ask":"So what is the effort you feel while learning something? If it goes away as you get good, what was it for?","choices":["The effort of learning is the thinking itself, and experts have stopped doing it","The effort of learning is how we trigger the adaptations that lead to the construction of expert networks.","The effort of learning is a sign you are not suited to the task"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>The effort is necessary to trigger the brain to build the networks that support expertise. The strain of learning is like the strain we feel at the gym when we signal our muscles to grow.</p>"},{"ask":"Here is the belief, then. Real thinking is slow and effortful, and the expert's fast, smooth version is just instinct. Do the grandmaster and the expert doctor support that view?","choices":["Effort is real thinking, and the expert's fluency is instinct","Our best “thinking” happens in the expert networks that run beneath awareness. They perform the complex calculations that our conscious mind cannot.","Effort and fluency count equally as thinking"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Our best “thinking” happens in the expert networks that run beneath awareness. They perform the complex calculations that our conscious mind cannot.</p><p>The expert is doing far more thinking, far faster, and yet we don’t feel it happening.</p>"},{"chunk":"<p>Even Einstein’s mental achievements are viewed like this. We see the finished insight and attribute it to a special brain, ignoring the years of work required to build the expert networks that could find it.</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">When you're learning, thinking is slow and effortful, so that's what we all decided real thinking must be. We look at an expert whose thinking has gone fast and smooth and call it natural ability.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">The book explains why expert thinking feels effortless, and why we undervalue it as a result.</p>"}],"next":{"id":"introspective-bias","name":"Introspective Bias","reason":"Where do these mistakes about thinking come from?"},"links":[{"id":"automaticity","name":"Automaticity"},{"id":"effort-inversion","name":"Effort Inversion"}],"game":null},"special-brain-story":{"name":"The Special Brain Story","steps":[{"label":"The Bias Line · The Impostor Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">The Special Brain Story</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">Why do you think some people are so much better at things than others?</p>"},{"ask":"Nobody believes some people were issued a finer liver, a better heart, or a more gifted spleen. And yet, think about how we talk about brains. Which organ gets the story that some people got a better one?","choices":["Every organ gets that story, in different ways","Only the brain gets the story that some people got a better one","The heart and the brain get that story"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Only the brain.</p><p>Of every organ in your body, the brain is the one and only one we talk about this way. Nobody has ever been told they have a second-rate pancreas and should manage their expectations accordingly.</p>"},{"chunk":"<p>Based on what we know about biology, genetics, and evolution, we would expect all brains to be roughly the same. Just like our other organs. Our similarities are what make us members of the same species.</p><p>Nor do we have evidence to suggest otherwise. For example, after an exhaustive pathological analysis, Albert Einstein’s brain wasn’t found to have any special bits that would explain his genius. In fact, it was smaller than average.</p>"},{"ask":"So where did the story that brains are the exception come from, if not from looking at brains?","choices":["The special brain story came from the brain science of the 1900s","The special brain story came from before anyone could study the brain at all. We built the story first, then tried to confirm that bias","The special brain story came from IQ testing"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>The story came long before anyone could look at a brain at all.</p><p>The idea that each head comes with a fixed ration of smart juice came long before we’d rigorously studied the brain. Now that we can look, we keep trying to make what we find fit that story.</p>"},{"ask":"But a story like that can stick around even if it’s not based on science. For example, suppose you believe you are not a math person. What do you do about math?","choices":["If you believe you are not a math person, you work harder at math","If you believe you are not a math person, you avoid math, and never do the training that would have built the skill","If you believe you are not a math person, nothing about your behavior changes"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>If you think being good at math requires being born with a math brain, then the obvious conclusion when you struggle is that you didn’t get one. So you avoid it.</p><p>You don’t do the work that builds a math brain, and the missing skill looks like proof the story was right. The special brain story manufactures its own evidence. It explains every failure in advance, so nobody bothers to ask whether the teaching was any good, and the failures keep right on coming.</p><p class=\"lf-cite\">Fixed-mindset avoidance (Dweck)</p>"},{"ask":"So, what actually determines what our brains are capable of?","choices":["What determines it is the brain each person was issued","What determines it is our learning path","What determines it is luck"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Our learning path is what determines what our brains are capable of, because our capabilities are built through learning.</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">The story says you were handed a certain kind of brain at birth, better or worse than the next person's, and that your job is to work out which one you got and stay in your lane. It's everywhere, it feels like plain common sense, and there has never been a shred of evidence for it.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">This is the story the whole book is written against.</p>"}],"next":{"id":"the-genius","name":"The Genius","reason":"If not a special brain, what built your abilities?"},"links":[{"id":"the-impostor","name":"The Impostor"},{"id":"uncommon-genius","name":"Uncommon Genius"},{"id":"introspective-bias","name":"Introspective Bias"}],"game":null},"the-impostor":{"name":"The Impostor","steps":[{"label":"The Impostor Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">The Impostor</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">When you pick one thing over another, how do you know why you picked it?</p>"},{"chunk":"<p>Forty shoppers were asked to pick the best of four pairs of stockings laid out in a row. What nobody told them is that the four pairs were identical.</p><p>Forty percent of them picked the pair on the far right and only twelve percent picked the one on the far left, with nearly three quarters choosing from the right-hand half of the table. Then the researchers asked the subjects why, and every shopper had an answer ready. All of their explanations were related to features of the stockings. Not one person mentioned where the pair was sitting on the table.</p><p>Furthermore, when the researchers suggested that position in the row might have had something to do with it, the shoppers looked at them as though they were out of their minds.</p><p class=\"lf-cite\">Nisbett &amp; Wilson, 1977</p>"},{"ask":"In people whose two brain halves have been surgically separated, one half can act for a reason the talking half cannot know. Ask the talking half why. What does it say?","choices":["The talking half answers honestly, saying it has no idea why it acted","The talking half instantly makes up a reason for the action, and fully believes it","The talking half goes silent"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>The talking part of the brain makes one up. And believes it wholeheartedly.</p><p>The talking half has no access whatsoever to why the other half did what it did, and it never once says so. What this reveals is that the talking half doesn’t care whether an explanation is true, only that it is believable.</p><p class=\"lf-cite\">Split-brain confabulation (Gazzaniga)</p>"},{"ask":"You have that same system. So here is the question. When the voice in your head tells you what you are and are not good at, what is it actually doing?","choices":["The voice is reporting honest self-knowledge","The voice is narrating, the way the shoppers narrated the stockings. It produces a confident story from whatever it can see","The voice is repeating what teachers told you"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>It’s concocting a story, just like it did with the stockings.</p><p>It was built to keep a running story of you, largely for managing how you look to other people, and it is very, very good at sounding certain. Accuracy was never in the job description.</p><p>This narrator is your Impostor, and he’s in every brain.</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">There's a part of your brain whose whole job is to narrate you, keeping a running story of who you are and what you're capable of. But that story is a fabrication.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">The Impostor is one of the book’s two title characters.</p>"}],"next":{"id":"unreliable-narrator","name":"The Unreliable Narrator","reason":"How reliable is the narrator in a healthy brain?"},"links":[{"id":"special-brain-story","name":"The Special Brain Story"},{"id":"capability-accessibility-model","name":"The Capability-Accessibility Model"},{"id":"the-genius","name":"The Genius"}],"game":{"name":"The Genius Diagnostic","url":"../diagnostic.html"}},"the-genius":{"name":"The Genius","steps":[{"label":"The Genius Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">The Genius</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">Which of your skills took the most lessons to learn?</p>"},{"ask":"Think about the hardest things you can do. Understand speech in a noisy room. Read faces. Walk. Speak a language with its grammar and idioms. Who taught you those?","choices":["Parents and teachers taught you those, mostly","Nobody taught you those. You built every one of them before anyone could have taught you","School taught you those, over many years"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Nobody taught you any of them.</p><p>A two-year-old learns language with no lessons and nobody explaining a single rule. Your brain finds the patterns straight out of the flood of speech in the room, reverse engineers the entire system, and builds it from scratch in their own brain.</p><p>The most advanced learning most humans ever do happens with no lessons at all.</p>"},{"ask":"In an experiment, preschoolers were handed a toy with several hidden functions in it. Some were shown one function first, as a little lesson. Some were just handed the toy with nothing said at all. The children who got no lesson went on to discover far more of what the toy could do. So what did the lesson do to the children who got one?","choices":["The lesson bored them, so they lost interest in the toy","The lesson answered the question for them, so they did the one thing they were shown and stopped looking","The lesson confused them, because one function made the others harder to find"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>The lesson answered the question, and the search closed.</p><p>The taught children did the thing they were shown and assumed that was it. The untaught children followed their curiosity to discover, so they kept going and found more.</p><p class=\"lf-cite\">Bonawitz et al., 2011</p>"},{"ask":"We tend to think that learning is something that involves teachers, classrooms, and formal instruction. What does the record of your own life say?","choices":["The record says the belief is true. Everything I know, I was taught","The record says the things I am best at, I taught myself, outside of the classroom","The record says it depends on the subject"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>The evidence shows that you're self-taught at precisely the things you're best at.</p><p>As a child, you learned the core human cognitive abilities without formal lessons. The adults weren't useless, but their job was never to install anything. Their job was to help create an environment that allowed your learning engine to build your knowledge and skills.</p><p>That engine is your Genius. It's still with you right now, and every station on this map is about how it works.</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">There's an engine in you that built every skill you've got without ever being told how. It learned to walk and talk and read faces while you were busy being a child. It runs on curiosity, it has no interest at all in grades, and it never left.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">The Genius is the book’s other title character.</p>"}],"next":{"id":"adult-learning","name":"Adult Learning","reason":"Does the learning engine keep working after childhood?"},"links":[{"id":"the-impostor","name":"The Impostor"},{"id":"automaticity","name":"Automaticity"},{"id":"progressive-composition","name":"Progressive Composition"}],"game":{"name":"The Genius Diagnostic","url":"../diagnostic.html"}},"adult-learning":{"name":"Adult Learning","steps":[{"label":"The Genius Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">Adult Learning</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">What’s the last new skill you started learning as an adult?</p>"},{"chunk":"<p>For most of the twentieth century, neuroscientists believed that the adult brain couldn’t change. Santiago Ramón y Cajal, often called the father of modern neuroscience, wrote that in the adult brain, the nerve paths are “something fixed, ended, immutable.”</p><p>The saying that you can’t teach an old dog new tricks seemed to have science behind it. If you hadn’t learned to speak Mandarin or throw a slider by the time you were thirty, you had missed your chance.</p><p class=\"lf-cite\">Ramón y Cajal, Degeneration and Regeneration of the Nervous System</p>"},{"ask":"In the 1980s, neuroscientist Michael Merzenich mapped the area of an adult monkey’s brain that receives sensation from its fingers. Each finger has its own patch of brain, sitting next to the patch for the finger beside it. His team then surgically joined two of the monkey’s fingers so they moved as one. Several weeks later, they mapped the brain again. The patches for the two joined fingers had merged into one. What does that tell you about the adult brain?","choices":["The adult brain rewired its map to match how the hand was now being used","The surgery damaged that part of the monkey’s brain","Monkey brains can change as adults, but human brains can’t"],"correct":0,"answer":"<p>The adult brain can change. The monkey’s brain had rewired its map to match the new shape of its hand, which the prevailing view said was impossible.</p><p>These results were initially met with skepticism. But experiment after experiment, including ones performed in humans, confirmed unequivocally that the adult brain is plastic.</p><p class=\"lf-cite\">Merzenich and colleagues, 1980s</p>"},{"ask":"A newborn’s brain has almost no model of the world yet. It can’t predict what a face will do next, what happens when a cup tips over, or what the sounds of speech mean. So its model is wrong about almost everything, and it rewires itself every day. By adulthood, your brain has built a model of the world that predicts what happens around you almost all the time. Your brain has also learned to control your body well. What does that mean for how much the adult brain rewires itself?","choices":["Nothing, because the brain rewires at the same rate for life","It should rewire much less, because a model that works gives the brain little reason to change","It should stop rewiring for good, because the model is finished"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>It should rewire much less. Your brain changes its wiring when its model of the world turns out to be wrong, or not good enough for what you’re trying to do. A newborn’s model is wrong all the time, so a young child’s brain is changing constantly. An adult’s model works almost all the time, so there’s much less that needs changing.</p><p>But the Genius never stops checking whether your brain’s maps and skills are still accurate and still good enough. That’s what Merzenich’s monkey showed. When its old finger map stopped matching its hand, its adult brain rewired the map. Adults change less because less needs changing, not because the brain has lost its ability to change.</p>"},{"ask":"Suppose that years ago you taught yourself a golf swing that slices the ball far to the right, and you’ve hit thousands of balls with it since. Now you want to hit the ball straight. Why is learning a straight swing now harder than it would have been in the first place?","choices":["Because your body is older now","Because the slicing swing now runs automatically","Because golf gets harder the more you play it"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Because the slicing swing now runs automatically. Once a skill is automated, it becomes your brain’s default, so every time you step up to the ball, your brain switches the old swing on. To learn the new swing, you first have to keep the old one from running.</p><p>The common name for an automated skill you don’t want is a bad habit, and after a few decades of learning, every adult has a few. That’s one reason learning can feel harder as an adult. The difficulty comes from a skill your brain has already built. The same learning process that automated the slice can automate a straight swing.</p>"},{"ask":"If you stopped walking tomorrow, your leg muscles would begin to shrink, and walking would get harder. Your body doesn’t spend energy maintaining what it isn’t using. Rewiring the brain also depends on its own biological machinery. What happens to that machinery when you stop learning new things?","choices":["Nothing. The brain’s ability to change stays the same no matter what","It weakens, so learning new things gets harder","It gets stronger from the rest"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>It weakens. When you stop learning, your brain stops maintaining the machinery it uses to rewire itself, so the same effort produces less change. In other words, plasticity itself is plastic: the less we learn, the harder learning becomes, and the more we learn, the easier it gets, no matter your age.</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">Your brain keeps its ability to change for your whole life. Adult brains change less mainly because less needs changing, and the more you keep learning, the easier learning stays.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">The book devotes a chapter to the evidence that the adult brain keeps its ability to change, and to the seven reasons it seems otherwise.</p>"}],"next":{"id":"genius-led-learning","name":"Genius-Led Learning","reason":"Which kind of learning builds lasting knowledge and skill?"},"links":[{"id":"the-genius","name":"The Genius"},{"id":"goldilocks-zone","name":"The Goldilocks Zone"},{"id":"special-brain-story","name":"The Special Brain Story"}],"game":null},"genius-led-learning":{"name":"Genius-Led Learning","steps":[{"label":"The Genius Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">Genius-Led Learning</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">Does passing a test mean you learned something?</p>"},{"ask":"Think about what you learned in school, and compare it with something you learned on your own because you wanted to, like a sport, a hobby, or an instrument. Which do you remember and use better today?","choices":["What I learned in school","What I learned on my own","About the same"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>For most people, it’s what they learned on their own. We forget most of what we studied in school, yet we can become masters of what we pursue outside it.</p><p>It’s the same brain in both cases. So the difference has to come from how the learning happened.</p>"},{"ask":"Research shows that effective learning needs three ingredients. First, intrinsic motivation, meaning you actually want to learn it. Second, active construction, meaning you build understanding by exploring and trying things yourself. Third, ongoing feedback, meaning you find out quickly whether each attempt worked. How well does a typical school class provide them?","choices":["It provides all three well","It provides one or two of them well","It provides very little of any of them"],"correct":2,"answer":"<p>Very little of any of them. In school, the subject is chosen for you, and the motivation comes from grades and rankings rather than from wanting to know. You mostly sit and receive information from lectures and textbooks instead of building it yourself. And feedback comes as tests every few weeks, designed to measure you rather than to help you improve.</p><p>The things you learned on your own had all three ingredients. You wanted to learn them, you learned by doing, and you could tell right away whether each attempt worked.</p>"},{"ask":"School motivates us mainly with grades, rankings, and the fear of getting answers wrong. Which part of you do those incentives appeal to: the part that wants to understand, or the part that cares about how you look to others?","choices":["The part that wants to understand","The part that cares about how you look to others","Both parts equally"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>The part that cares about how you look to others, which the book calls the Impostor. Grades are a measure of status. Your Genius, the part of you that actually knows how to learn, doesn’t care about them. It’s driven by curiosity.</p><p>Research shows that learning for external rewards like grades also reduces natural curiosity over time. Leonardo da Vinci recognized the cost of learning without desire centuries ago: “Study without desire spoils the memory, and it retains nothing that it takes in.”</p>"},{"ask":"Compared with children, adults who face something new to learn tend to wait for instructions, look for the one right answer quickly, avoid trial and error, and give up when it gets hard. Where do these habits come from?","choices":["They come with getting older","They were learned, mostly in school","They’re part of an adult’s personality"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>They were learned, mostly in school. For more than a decade, school rewarded exactly these habits: follow the instructions, find the right answer, avoid mistakes. So as adults, we keep using some of the least effective ways to learn.</p><p>Because these are learned habits, they can be replaced with the ones that work: following your curiosity, exploring, and learning through trial and error.</p>"},{"ask":"Most of us judge whether we’ve learned something by whether we passed the test. Think back to the subjects you studied for tests in school. Does passing the test mean you learned it?","choices":["Passing the test means the knowledge is built and will last","Passing the test shows you could produce the answers that day, and most of that knowledge fades","Passing the test means you learned the parts you got right"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Passing a test doesn’t mean you learned it. It shows that you could produce the answers on the day of the test, and knowledge built for a grade mostly fades once the test is over.</p><p>Knowledge that lasts gets built the way you learned the things you taught yourself: out of curiosity, by doing, with feedback all along the way.</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">There are two ways to learn, and they have very different success rates. Genius-led learning is driven by curiosity, with the goal of real understanding. Impostor-led learning is driven by status, with the goal of getting the grade. The first builds knowledge and skill that lasts. The second mostly produces knowledge that is shallow and temporary.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">The book compares Genius-led and Impostor-led learning, and explains why traditional schooling relies on the second.</p>"}],"next":{"id":"goldilocks-zone","name":"The Goldilocks Zone","reason":"How hard should practice be for learning to happen fastest?"},"links":[{"id":"the-genius","name":"The Genius"},{"id":"the-impostor","name":"The Impostor"}],"game":null},"progressive-composition":{"name":"Progressive Composition","steps":[{"label":"The Machinery Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">Progressive Composition</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">When you learned to read, what did you learn first?</p>"},{"ask":"Babies everywhere reach the motor milestones in the same order: head control, then sitting, then standing, then walking. No baby has ever walked before learning to sit up. Why is the order always the same?","choices":["Because parents teach the milestones in that order","Because each milestone depends on the skills built in the one before it","Because walking is simply the hardest skill"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Because each milestone depends on the skills built in the one before it. You can’t walk before you can balance sitting up. Each new motor skill is assembled from the ones already in place, which is why the order is the same in every country on earth.</p>"},{"ask":"Children learn words in a set order too: nouns first, then verbs, then adjectives, then adverbs. But verbs aren’t any harder to pronounce than nouns. So why do children learn nouns first?","choices":["Because nouns are shorter words","Because nouns name concrete things, which makes them the easiest words to understand","Because children are taught nouns first in school"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Because children learn to say the words they understand, and nouns are the easiest to understand. A noun names a concrete object that a child can see and touch. A verb names an action, which is harder to picture.</p><p>Adjectives and adverbs come later because each one describes another word. An adjective describes a thing, so a child needs nouns first. An adverb describes how an action is done, so a child needs verbs first.</p>"},{"ask":"Walking and talking are very different skills, but in both, each new skill is built on the ones before it. What does that tell you about how skills are built?","choices":["Walking and talking are each built by their own separate process","Walking and talking are built by a similar underlying learning process","The resemblance between walking and talking is a coincidence"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>It tells you that walking and talking are built by a similar underlying learning process.</p><p>You learn simple pieces, practice them until they run on their own, combine them into bigger pieces, and then repeat the cycle. That’s how every skill you have was built, from walking to reading to math. This is the process of progressive composition.</p>"},{"chunk":"<p>With a physical skill like walking, you can watch the pieces come together. With skills like reading or chess, all of that building happens inside your head where no one can see it, so it’s natural to think those skills are built some other way. But they’re built in the same way.</p><p>When you learned to read, you combined lines into letters, letters into words, and words into sentences. A banjo player combines single finger strokes into short patterns called rolls, rolls into musical phrases called licks, and licks into whole tunes. A chess player learns how each chess piece moves, then how a few pieces work together, then whole positions. In each case, the next skill can only be built once the one before it runs on its own.</p><video src=\"media/progressive-composition-stack.mp4?v=2\" poster=\"media/progressive-composition-stack.jpg?v=2\" data-play-visible muted playsinline preload=\"auto\" aria-label=\"Animation: lines build letters, letters build a word, words build a sentence. Beside it, banjo finger strokes build rolls, licks and a tune, and chess pieces build groups, patterns and a position.\" title=\"Tap to replay\" onclick=\"this.currentTime=0;this.play()\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;max-width:min(100%,46vh);margin:0 auto;height:auto;border:1px solid var(--rule,#e0dcd4);border-radius:4px;background:#fafaf8;cursor:pointer\"></video>"},{"ask":"At eighteen months, a typical child can use about ten words, enough to make five to ten different sentences. By age five, the same child knows about three thousand words and could make around fifty billion different sentences. Vocabulary grew about three hundred times. Why did the number of possible sentences grow by billions?","choices":["Because older children have bigger brains","Because each word a child uses automatically can be combined with every other word they know","Because older children practice speaking more"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Because once a word runs automatically, it’s available to combine with every other word you know. So each new word multiplies the number of sentences you can make.</p><p>The same thing happens in every domain. Automated movements combine into new actions, and automated ideas combine into new thoughts. Each skill you automate becomes a piece you can combine with every skill you build after it.</p>"},{"ask":"We tend to think that talented people can skip the basics and start at an advanced level. Think about the babies learning to walk and the children learning words. Does anyone skip the steps?","choices":["Some people skip the basics and start at an advanced level","Nobody skips the steps. Every skill is built from simpler skills, in order","People skip steps in physical skills but not in mental ones"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Nobody skips the steps. Expertise in any domain is built from simpler skills, and each one depends on the skills below it. Some people move through the steps earlier or faster, because they get a head start, they practice more or practice at the right level of difficulty, but everyone who reaches mastery goes through every step.</p><p>The Genius is a structural engineer, building the neural networks that support your cognitive skills. And you can’t build the penthouse of a skyscraper before you’ve poured the concrete for the lobby, because each floor rests on the floors below it.</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">Every skill gets built the same way. You learn simple pieces, practice them until they run on their own, and then combine them into bigger pieces and repeat. The book calls this progressive composition, and it’s how walking, talking, reading, and math all get built.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">The book introduces progressive composition as the universal pattern of learning, the same in every domain.</p>"}],"next":{"id":"goldilocks-zone","name":"The Goldilocks Zone","reason":"How difficult does practice need to be for new skills to get built?"},"links":[{"id":"automaticity","name":"Automaticity"},{"id":"the-genius","name":"The Genius"}],"game":null},"goldilocks-zone":{"name":"The Goldilocks Zone","steps":[{"label":"The Machinery Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">The Goldilocks Zone</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">How often should you get it right when you practice something?</p>"},{"ask":"Lifting a 1-pound weight ten times won’t make your muscles grow. Neither will trying and failing to lift a 1,000-pound weight. So what kind of lifting will?","choices":["Lifting the 1-pound weight many more times","Lifting a weight you can manage five times, then struggling to lift it a sixth and seventh time","Trying the 1,000-pound weight every day until you can move it"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>The weight you can lift five times and struggle with after that is the one that builds muscle. The struggle tells your body that you need strength you don’t have yet, so building more muscle is worth the resources.</p><p>The 1-pound weight sends no signal, because you can already lift it easily. The 1,000-pound weight is so far beyond what you can do that you can’t produce any useful effort against it.</p>"},{"ask":"Learning works the same way, except the brain is solving problems instead of lifting weights. If every problem you practice is one you can already solve, what happens in your brain?","choices":["Your skills keep growing at the same rate, because practice always helps","Very little changes, because your current skills are already enough","It starts building networks for harder problems"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Very little changes. If you can already solve every problem you practice, your brain gets little signal that your current skills aren’t enough, so it has little reason to build anything new. And if you can’t solve any of them, it has nothing to build on.</p><p>Learning happens when you can solve some of the problems but not all of them. The failures signal that change is needed, and the successes show your brain which patterns to keep. That’s the Goldilocks Zone.</p>"},{"ask":"Research on how people learn best has found a sweet spot for how often you should succeed while you practice. Based on what you’ve just seen, which range makes the most sense?","choices":["Close to 100 percent, so you learn to do it right","Between about 50 and 85 percent","Below 25 percent, so the practice stays challenging"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Between about 50 and 85 percent, which means succeeding most of the time, but not every time. That gives your brain enough errors to signal that change is needed, and enough successes to show which patterns should become permanent.</p><p>In the Goldilocks Zone, practice isn’t so easy that you’re bored, or so hard that you’re frustrated.</p><p class=\"lf-cite\">Wilson et al., 2019 (optimal success rate near 85 percent)</p>"},{"ask":"Suppose you find the Goldilocks Zone for a skill you’re learning and keep practicing there. A few weeks later, the same practice feels easy. What happened?","choices":["The practice somehow got easier on its own","You built the skills it was challenging you to build, so your Goldilocks Zone moved","You lost interest in it"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>You built the skills the practice was challenging you to build, so your Goldilocks Zone moved. Problems that were once too hard become easy once the skills they need run automatically.</p><p>If you’re learning, your Goldilocks Zone is always moving. To keep growing, you have to keep making the practice harder as you improve.</p>"},{"ask":"Most of us feel good about practice that goes smoothly, where we get nearly everything right. Given the weights and the success rates, is that the kind of practice that builds skill?","choices":["Smooth practice builds skill, because getting things right means you’re learning","Smooth practice builds very little, because your brain gets little signal to change","Smooth practice builds skill in some areas and not others"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Practice that goes perfectly builds very little. The mistakes you make at the edge of your ability are the signal that tells your brain to build new networks.</p><p>Babies work this way without being told. When rolling over is in a baby’s Goldilocks Zone, the baby works at it relentlessly, failing again and again until it’s learned, and then moves on to the next skill.</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">Learning happens in a specific range of difficulty: hard enough that you fail some of the time, and easy enough that you succeed most of the time. The book calls this the Goldilocks Zone. Practice there should feel challenging, but attainable.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">The book describes how to find your Goldilocks Zone for any skill you want to learn.</p>"}],"next":{"id":"implicit-learning","name":"Implicit Learning","reason":"What about learning that happens without you noticing?"},"links":[{"id":"progressive-composition","name":"Progressive Composition"},{"id":"the-genius","name":"The Genius"}],"game":null},"implicit-learning":{"name":"Implicit Learning","steps":[{"label":"The Machinery Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">Implicit Learning</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">Can you know something without being able to explain it?</p>"},{"ask":"Read these two phrases: “a big red barn” and “a red big barn.” One sounds right and the other sounds wrong. Can you state the rule that tells you which is which?","choices":["Yes. I could explain the rule easily","No. I know which one is right, but I can’t say why","No, because there isn’t a rule"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Most people can’t. English puts adjectives in a strict order: opinion, then size, age, shape, color, origin, material, and purpose. That’s why “a lovely little old green French whittling knife” sounds normal, and any other order sounds wrong.</p><p>Almost nobody is taught this rule, and yet native speakers follow it almost without error.</p>"},{"ask":"If nobody taught you the rule, and you can’t state it, how did it get into your brain?","choices":["You were born knowing it","Your brain worked out the pattern from hearing thousands of examples, without you noticing","You learned it in school and forgot the lesson"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Your brain worked it out from years of hearing English spoken. It detected the pattern in thousands of examples and built it into your language networks, all without your awareness.</p><p>This is called implicit learning: learning that happens without awareness, and produces knowledge you can use but can’t put into words. Much of what you know about language, faces, movement, and social life was learned this way.</p>"},{"ask":"Every healthy child learns to speak their native language, grammar and all, without formal lessons. Nobody ever says a child has a “talent for English.” But when a child picks out songs on the piano or takes to numbers without being taught, we call it a gift. What’s the difference between the two?","choices":["Language is simple for the brain, while music and math are complex","There’s no difference in how they’re learned, but we don’t see the exposure behind the second one","Language is built into the brain at birth, while music and math aren’t"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>There’s no difference in how they’re learned. Learning a language and picking out songs on the piano both come from the same kind of implicit learning, driven by what the child pays attention to. Nobody calls a child’s English a gift, because every child learns a language. We call the piano a gift because fewer children do it, and we don’t see the hours of exposure that built it.</p><p>When we can’t see how a skill was learned, it looks like it came from nowhere. That’s why implicit learning is so easy to mistake for talent.</p>"},{"ask":"We tend to believe two things: if we know something, we can explain it, and people who pick up skills without instruction were born with a gift. What do the barn and the child at the piano say about those beliefs?","choices":["Both beliefs hold. Knowledge you can’t explain isn’t real, and gifts are real","Neither belief holds. Much of what we know was learned without awareness, and what looks like a gift is learning nobody saw","The first belief holds, but the second doesn’t"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Neither belief holds up. A large share of what you know was learned implicitly, and you use it every day without being able to explain it. What we call talent is the result of the same kind of learning, built from exposure that nobody counted as practice.</p><p>Every healthy brain comes equipped to learn this way, and abilities are what that learning builds.</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">Much of what you know, you learned without realizing it, and you can’t put it into words. You may not even realize you know it. Your brain picks up patterns from experience and builds them into skills without anyone teaching you the rules. When we don’t see that learning happen, we usually call the result a “gift”.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">The book explains how learning we don’t see gets mistaken for natural talent.</p>"}],"next":{"id":"automaticity","name":"Automaticity","reason":"How do practiced skills come to run without conscious effort?"},"links":[{"id":"the-genius","name":"The Genius"},{"id":"special-brain-story","name":"The Special Brain Story"}],"game":{"name":"The Secret Grammar","url":"../secret-grammar.html"}},"bottleneck-chunking":{"name":"The Bottleneck and Chunking","steps":[{"label":"The Machinery Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">The Bottleneck and Chunking</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">How do people with great memories remember so much?</p>"},{"chunk":"<p>How many random digits can you repeat back after hearing them read once? For most people, it’s about seven, and that’s only because we can repeat the digits to ourselves and group them. On its own, the conscious mind can hold only about four items at a time, a limit the book calls the conscious bottleneck.</p>"},{"ask":"Read this string of twelve letters once, then look away and try to say it back: A B F I M S B I U I A C. Now try this one: F B I C I A U S A I B M. Both strings use the same twelve letters, three times what your conscious mind can hold. Why is the second one so much easier?","choices":["You’d already had one try, so the second string got the benefit of practice","You read the second string as four abbreviations you already know, so there are only four things to hold","The second string has a rhythm that makes it easier to say"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>You read the second string as four abbreviations you already know: FBI, CIA, USA, and IBM. So instead of twelve letters, you only had to hold four items, which is right at the limit of the conscious bottleneck.</p><p>Each abbreviation is a chunk, a group of items your brain has learned to treat as one. Your memory capacity was the same for both strings.</p>"},{"chunk":"<p>You’ve been building chunks like these since you learned to read. At first, a letter was just a set of lines, and each line was a separate item for your conscious mind. With practice, you built networks in your brain that recognize a whole letter as a single item, and then a whole word.</p><p>Now a word like THINK is a single item, even though it’s made of twelve lines. Your conscious mind still takes in only one item at a time and holds only about four. What learning changes is how much each item contains.</p><video src=\"media/bottleneck-chunking-reading.mp4?v=2\" poster=\"media/bottleneck-chunking-reading.jpg\" data-play-visible muted playsinline preload=\"auto\" aria-label=\"Animation: at first each line of a letter is a separate item. With practice, a whole letter becomes one item, then a whole word. The word THINK becomes one item made of twelve lines.\" title=\"Tap to replay\" onclick=\"this.currentTime=0;this.play()\" style=\"display:block;width:100%;max-width:min(100%,46vh);margin:0 auto;height:auto;border:1px solid var(--rule,#e0dcd4);border-radius:4px;background:#fafaf8;cursor:pointer\"></video>"},{"ask":"Now imagine giving the second string to a ten-year-old who reads well but has never heard of the FBI, the CIA, or IBM. How would they do with it?","choices":["As well as you, because it’s the same twelve letters","Not much better than with the first string, because to them most of it is random letters","Better than you, because the meanings wouldn’t distract them"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Not much better than with the first string. They’d spot USA, but without the other three abbreviations stored in their brain, they have ten items to hold: nine separate letters plus USA. That’s more than twice what the conscious mind can manage.</p><p>A chunk only works if you’ve learned it. That’s why experts can remember far more inside their own field, with the same capacity as everyone else, and why that advantage disappears when they step outside it.</p>"},{"ask":"IQ tests include tasks like repeating back strings of digits, and IQ scores are most closely linked to working memory. Given the two strings of letters, what do those scores mostly measure?","choices":["How intelligent a person is across every area of life","Basic resources like working memory, which help with learning but aren’t intelligence","How hard a person tried on the day of the test"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>IQ scores mostly reflect basic resources like working memory, which help with learning but aren’t intelligence. While there’s a small amount of variation in these resources from one person to the next, they pale in comparison to what we can achieve through learning, as the two strings of letters show.</p><p>Knowing four abbreviations let you hold twelve letters in a space that holds about four.</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">Your conscious mind can hold only a few items at once, and that limit is small for everyone. Experts don’t have a larger capacity. Through learning, they pack more meaning into each item, and that skill only works in the area where they built it.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">The book explains why IQ tests track basic resources like working memory, and why those resources matter far less than what we’ve learned.</p>"}],"next":{"id":"everyday-genius","name":"Everyday Genius","reason":"How much does your brain do without you noticing?"},"links":[{"id":"automaticity","name":"Automaticity"},{"id":"domain-specific-intelligence","name":"Domain-Specific Intelligence"},{"id":"trained-reasoning","name":"Trained Reasoning"}],"game":{"name":"The Bottleneck","url":"../bottleneck.html"}},"everyday-genius":{"name":"Everyday Genius","steps":[{"label":"The Machinery Line · The Intelligence Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">Everyday Genius</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">What’s the most complicated thing you did today?</p>"},{"chunk":"<p>When computer scientists started trying to build thinking machines in the mid-twentieth century, they assumed that the problems humans find hardest, like chess and math, would be the hardest to program. Those turned out to be the easy ones. Pocket calculators have existed since the 1970s, and a computer beat the world chess champion in 1997.</p><p>What turned out to be hard were skills every toddler has, like walking across a cluttered room without falling and recognizing a face from any angle. Robots still struggle with tasks like folding laundry. This is known as Moravec’s Paradox.</p><p class=\"lf-cite\">Moravec’s Paradox</p>"},{"ask":"Why would the skills that feel easiest to us turn out to be the hardest to program?","choices":["Because computers are bad at anything physical","Because those skills are extremely complex, and they only feel easy because we’ve mastered them so completely","Because nobody tried hard enough to program them"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Because they solve some of the most complex problems a brain ever faces. They feel easy because we’ve automated them so completely that we don’t feel any of the work. Easy does not equal simple.</p><p>The computer scientists made the same mistake the rest of us make: they judged how complex a skill is by how hard it feels to do.</p>"},{"ask":"Think back to your first time behind the wheel, when talking while you drove was impossible. Now you’re driving home while having a conversation with a friend in the passenger seat. To pull that off, you’re tracking the cars around you, obeying traffic signals, and adjusting your speed, while also decoding speech, following the story, tracking tone of voice, and choosing what to say. Productivity experts often say humans can’t multitask. How does that fit with your drive home?","choices":["It fits. This isn’t really multitasking","It doesn’t fit. This is an extraordinary feat of multitasking, made possible by skills that run automatically and in parallel","It only works because driving is easy"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>This is an extraordinary feat of multitasking. It’s possible because both driving and conversation run in expert networks that don’t need your conscious attention. It took computer scientists decades to get machines to do even one of those things.</p><p>There was a time you couldn’t do either one. You built every one of those skills through learning.</p>"},{"ask":"Walking, talking, and recognizing faces solve some of the hardest problems a brain faces. So why doesn’t anyone call a toddler a genius?","choices":["Because what toddlers do isn’t actually difficult","Because nearly every toddler can do it, so it isn’t rare","Because toddlers are born able to do it"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Because it isn’t rare. When we call something genius, we usually mean two things: it solved a very difficult problem, and few other people could have done it. Walking, talking, and recognizing faces easily pass the first test. They fail only the second, because nearly everyone can do them.</p><p>If only one person on earth could talk or recognize faces, we would call that person a genius.</p>"},{"ask":"Real intelligence, the thinking goes, is rare, and walking and talking are basic skills anyone can do. Which view fits Moravec’s Paradox and the drive home?","choices":["Intelligence shows up only in rare achievements like chess and advanced math","The everyday skills everyone has are some of the most intelligent things a brain does","Everyday skills are complex, but they don’t count as intelligence"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>The everyday skills everyone has are some of the most intelligent things a brain does. They solve extraordinarily difficult problems, and they were built by the same learning process that produces every achievement we call genius.</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">The skills you use without thinking, like walking across a room, understanding speech, and recognizing faces, are solutions to some of the most complex problems a brain ever faces. We don’t call them genius only because nearly everyone has them.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">The book uses the term common genius for the extraordinary skills that nearly everyone has.</p>"}],"next":{"id":"domain-specific-intelligence","name":"Domain-Specific Intelligence","reason":"What is intelligence, and why can’t it be ranked on one scale?"},"links":[{"id":"automaticity-blindness","name":"Automaticity Blindness"},{"id":"uncommon-genius","name":"Uncommon Genius"},{"id":"special-brain-story","name":"The Special Brain Story"}],"game":null},"uncommon-genius":{"name":"Uncommon Genius","steps":[{"label":"The Machinery Line · The Intelligence Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">Uncommon Genius</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">What do you picture when you hear the word genius?</p>"},{"chunk":"<p>Albert Einstein didn’t start speaking until around age two, and his parents and teachers worried about his speech for much of his childhood. Some of his teachers found him slow and dreamy, and he hated the rote drilling of his schools.</p><p>What he did have was curiosity. When he was five, his father showed him a compass, and he became fascinated by the invisible force that always pointed the needle north. He spent years thinking about the nature of light. At twenty-three, after failing to land a university job, he took an undemanding job as a patent clerk, which left him time to think about those questions every day.</p>"},{"ask":"Einstein spent those years reading papers, talking with other physicists, and working through thought experiments, and all of it built skills in his brain. The questions he was working on were ones almost nobody else was asking. What would you expect that path to produce?","choices":["The same skills other physicists of his time had","An unusual set of skills, able to solve problems nobody else could","Very little, because unusual paths rarely lead anywhere"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>An unusual set of skills. Because his learning path was unique, the set of problems his brain could solve was unique too. His discoveries only came within reach after that learning had been done. Before then, they were impossible for his brain, as they would be for anyone’s.</p><p>What separates uncommon genius from common genius is uniqueness, not complexity.</p>"},{"ask":"When plague closed Cambridge in 1665, Isaac Newton, then twenty-two, went home to his family’s farm, studied on his own, and invented calculus. Charles Darwin dropped out of medical school and spent five years on a ship collecting specimens. Emily Dickinson barely left her house for decades, and invented a new form of poetry. What do these three have in common?","choices":["Each of them had an unusually powerful brain","Each of them followed their own curiosity down a path few others took","Each of them had excellent schooling"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Each of them followed their own curiosity. That created an unusual learning path, and an unusual learning path builds an unusual set of skills.</p><p>Many of the people we call geniuses learned this way, outside the schools and institutions of their time. What they have in common is how they learned.</p>"},{"ask":"What about child prodigies, like a five-year-old who plays chess at a very high level? Doesn’t that prove some brains are special from the start?","choices":["Yes. Prodigies are born with special brains","No. What’s unusual about a prodigy is their interest, and the practice it leads to","No. Prodigies are just lucky"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>A prodigy’s brain isn’t what’s unusual. What’s unusual is their interests. A five-year-old obsessed with chess instead of Pokémon is rare, and that obsession leads to thousands of hours of chess while other children are building different skills.</p><p>That’s why prodigies seem miraculous compared with other five-year-olds, but not compared with people who’ve had the same amount of practice.</p>"},{"ask":"The Special Brain Story says that genius requires a special brain, and that most people don’t have what it takes. Think about what Einstein, Newton, Darwin, and Dickinson had in common. Where did their genius come from?","choices":["Their genius came from special brains, and the unusual paths were a side effect","Their genius came from unusual learning paths, built by the same learning process everyone has","Their genius came from copious amounts of ginkgo biloba"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Their genius came from where their curiosity led them. The learning process that built your everyday skills is the same one that built Einstein’s discoveries. What made his work uncommon was the path his curiosity took, and the unique set of skills that path built.</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">The breakthroughs we celebrate come from the same learning process that built your everyday skills. What makes them uncommon is the learning path. Someone spends years following an unusual curiosity, builds an unusual set of skills, and reaches solutions nobody else could.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">The book argues that what we call genius comes from a learning path, and uses Einstein’s life as its central example.</p>"}],"next":{"id":"prediction-engine","name":"The Prediction Engine","reason":"How does your brain use what it has learned to predict what comes next?"},"links":[{"id":"everyday-genius","name":"Everyday Genius"},{"id":"special-brain-story","name":"The Special Brain Story"},{"id":"solution-space","name":"Solution Space"}],"game":null},"domain-specific-intelligence":{"name":"Domain-Specific Intelligence","steps":[{"label":"The Intelligence Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">Domain-Specific Intelligence</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">Think of the smartest person you know. Are they smart at everything?</p>"},{"ask":"A London cab driver and a tourist in a rental car both need to drive from Paddington Station to the Tower of London. The cab driver is there in half an hour. The tourist, working from a paper map, takes an hour and a half after several wrong turns. Both of them got there. Whose solution showed more intelligence?","choices":["Neither, since both of them reached the Tower","The cab driver’s, because it reached the goal far more efficiently","The tourist’s, because it took more thinking"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>The cab driver’s. Both of them reached the goal, but the cab driver’s solution used far less time and effort. That’s what intelligence is: how well you solve problems to reach your goals. More effective and more efficient solutions demonstrate more intelligence.</p><p>And the cab driver built that intelligence. To get a license, London cab drivers spend three to four years learning the roughly 25,000 streets in the center of the city, a test known as the Knowledge.</p>"},{"ask":"Humans are far better than bats at sharing thoughts through spoken language. Bats are far better than humans at navigating a dark cave by echolocation. So which is more intelligent?","choices":["Humans, because language is the harder problem","Bats, because echolocation is the harder problem","Neither. Each is more intelligent at its own problems"],"correct":2,"answer":"<p>Neither. Humans are more intelligent at sharing thoughts, and bats are more intelligent at navigating in the dark. Intelligence isn’t one general quality that a person or a species has more or less of across the board.</p><p>Asking whether one species is more intelligent than another is like asking whether a submarine is better at “transportation” than a helicopter.</p>"},{"ask":"IQ reduces intelligence to a single number meant to rank everyone from least to most intelligent. Given the bats and the humans, what’s the problem with that?","choices":["There’s no problem. A single number is a fair summary","Intelligence depends on the problem being solved, so no single number can capture it","The number just needs to be measured more carefully"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>The problem is that intelligence depends on the problem being solved, so it can’t be reduced to one score. An IQ score reflects how well someone solves the particular kinds of problems on the test, at the time they take it.</p><p>Humans are also the only animals who could score well on an IQ test, because we designed the test around the kinds of problems humans are good at. A bat would fail it, even though bats solve problems no human can.</p>"},{"ask":"We assume that someone brilliant in one field will be sharp in every field. In 1993, Michael Jordan, the best basketball player in the world, retired from basketball to play professional baseball. He spent the 1994 season in the minor leagues, batted .202, and never made it to the major leagues. Does brilliance carry over from one field to another?","choices":["Brilliance carries over. A brilliant person is sharp in every field","Brilliance stays in the field where it was built","Brilliance mostly carries over, with a few exceptions"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Brilliance stays in the field where it was built. Jordan brought speed, strength, and a lifetime of athletic training to baseball, and those helped. But hitting a baseball is its own problem, and the skills that solve it are built by years of hitting baseballs. Intelligence is always specific to a domain, and it’s built by learning in that domain. An expert in one field is a beginner in every field they haven’t studied.</p><p>That’s why we’re so often surprised when a brilliant scientist makes poor decisions about money, or a chess champion struggles to run their own life. Those cases are exactly what you’d expect when intelligence is built one domain at a time.</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">Intelligence is the ability to solve problems to reach your goals, and it’s always specific to the problems you’ve learned to solve. There’s no general quality that makes a person smart at everything, and no single scale that ranks people from least to most intelligent.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">The book defines intelligence as the ability to solve problems to reach your goals, and shows why that ability is always specific to a domain.</p>"}],"next":{"id":"uncommon-genius","name":"Uncommon Genius","reason":"Where do the achievements we call genius come from?"},"links":[{"id":"bottleneck-chunking","name":"The Bottleneck and Chunking"},{"id":"trained-reasoning","name":"Trained Reasoning"},{"id":"special-brain-story","name":"The Special Brain Story"}],"game":null},"prediction-engine":{"name":"The Prediction Engine","steps":[{"label":"The Intelligence Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">The Prediction Engine</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">When someone tosses you a ball, when do your hands start moving?</p>"},{"ask":"Read this sentence: “She opened her umbrella because it had started to ___.” Before you reached the blank, you probably already knew what word was coming. How could you know?","choices":["Because you had read that exact sentence before","Because your brain was predicting the next word, based on patterns it has learned","Because you read ahead to the end of the line"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Your brain was predicting the next word. It uses the patterns it has learned from years of reading and listening to prepare for what’s likely to come next.</p><p>That’s a big part of why reading and listening feel effortless, and why you can often finish someone else’s sentence.</p>"},{"ask":"Albert Pujols, one of the best hitters in baseball history, regularly hit fastballs thrown at over 90 miles per hour. In a 2004 exhibition, he faced the Olympic softball pitcher Jennie Finch, who throws underhand, and he couldn’t make contact. When researchers tested his reaction time, it was only a little above average for a college student. So what made him such a great hitter?","choices":["Faster reflexes than other people","Years of learning to predict where a pitch was going from the pitcher’s motion","Better eyesight"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Years of learning to predict. A fastball reaches the plate in about four-tenths of a second, too fast to wait and see where it’s going, so hitters start their swing based on what the pitcher’s body and the ball’s first few feet of flight tell them. Pujols had spent years building that prediction skill against baseball pitchers.</p><p>Finch’s underhand motion looked nothing like what he had trained on, so his predictions didn’t work, and his skill didn’t carry over. Much of what looks like fast reflexes is prediction built through learning, and it only works in the domain where it was built.</p><p class=\"lf-cite\">Pujols vs. Jennie Finch, 2004</p>"},{"ask":"You’re walking up a staircase in the dark, and you step for one more stair that isn’t there. What happens?","choices":["Nothing. You just keep walking","You feel a jolt, because your brain had predicted a step","You fall down"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>You feel a jolt. Your brain had predicted another step and had already prepared your leg for it. When the prediction turns out to be wrong, your brain registers the error instantly.</p><p>Those errors are how your brain learns. Each time a prediction fails, your brain uses the difference between what it expected and what happened to improve the next prediction.</p>"},{"ask":"It feels as if your brain takes in the world first and then works out what it means. In the umbrella sentence, the pitch, and the missing stair, which came first?","choices":["Taking in the world came first, and working out what it meant came after","Prediction came first. Your brain prepared for what was likely before it happened","Prediction came first only for the expert hitter"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Prediction came first. Your brain is constantly predicting what’s about to happen, based on what it has learned, and correcting itself when it’s wrong. Understanding a sentence, hitting a ball, and climbing stairs all depend on predictions built through learning.</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">Your brain doesn’t just take in what’s happening and then react to it. It constantly predicts what’s about to happen, based on everything it has learned, and checks those predictions against what actually happens. Much of what we call skill is prediction built through learning.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">The book describes pattern recognition as the brain’s core competency.</p>"}],"next":{"id":"capability-accessibility-model","name":"The Capability-Accessibility Model","reason":"Why can’t you always reach what you know?"},"links":[{"id":"goldilocks-zone","name":"The Goldilocks Zone"},{"id":"domain-specific-intelligence","name":"Domain-Specific Intelligence"}],"game":null},"routing-architecture":{"name":"Routing Architecture","steps":[{"label":"The Access Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">Routing Architecture</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">Have you ever had the answer to a problem come to you after you’d stopped thinking about it?</p>"},{"chunk":"<p>For years, the chemist Dmitri Mendeleev tried to work out how the chemical elements were related. On long train rides, he wrote each element on a card and tried arranging the cards so that a pattern would appear. It never did.</p><p>According to the story he later told, he fell asleep at his desk one day after obsessing over the problem for days. In a dream, he saw a table where all the elements fell into place. When he woke up, he wrote it down. It became the periodic table.</p><p class=\"lf-cite\">Mendeleev, as told by Oliver Sacks</p>"},{"ask":"Mendeleev didn’t learn anything new while he was asleep. So how could his sleeping brain find something his waking brain couldn’t?","choices":["He was consciously working on the problem in his dream","Sleep let parts of his brain connect that couldn’t while he was awake","It was a lucky guess"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Sleep changed how information flowed through his brain. Mendeleev had spent years building the knowledge he needed. What he still needed was to connect that knowledge in a new way, and the pattern of brain activity during sleep made those connections possible. Sleep didn’t change what he knew. It changed what his brain could get to.</p><p>The book calls each of these patterns of brain activity a routing architecture. Each mode your brain operates in, from focused work to sleep, has its own routing architecture.</p>"},{"chunk":"<p>The book compares this to a subway system. During the day, one set of stations and tracks is open. At night, a different set opens, and you can reach places you couldn’t get to during the day.</p><p>Your brain works the same way. Focused work, mind-wandering, and sleep each open some routes between brain regions and close others. The knowledge in your brain stays the same. What changes is which parts of it can connect.</p>"},{"ask":"In one study, people spent a few minutes listing unusual uses for everyday objects, like a brick. Then they took a break. Some spent it on a demanding task, and others did an easy task that let their minds wander. When they came back to the same objects, the mind-wanderers came up with far more new uses. Why?","choices":["The easy task left them less tired","Mind-wandering put their brains in a mode that could reach different ideas","The easy task gave them more time to think about the objects"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Mind-wandering shifted their brains into a different mode, called the default mode, which has its own routing architecture. That mode connected ideas that stayed apart while they were focused.</p><p>It wasn’t rest alone. A third group that simply rested during the break didn’t improve. It’s the same reason a name you couldn’t remember comes to you later, once you’ve stopped trying.</p><p class=\"lf-cite\">Baird et al., 2012</p>"},{"ask":"An expert performer under intense pressure suddenly can’t do something they’ve done thousands of times. An hour later, the skill is back. Given what we’ve reviewed, what did the pressure do?","choices":["The pressure erased the skill for a while","The pressure shifted the brain into a mode that blocked access to the skill","The pressure revealed that the skill was never solid"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>It shifted the brain into a different mode. Under pressure, the parts of the brain that monitor and judge you become more active, and they interfere with the expert networks that normally run the skill on their own.</p><p>This is the same thing that happens when your mind goes blank during a toast. The skill was in the performer’s brain the whole time, but the brain state they were in couldn’t reach it.</p><p class=\"lf-cite\">Choking under pressure (Beilock &amp; Carr, 2001)</p>"},{"ask":"We tend to think that if we can’t come up with something, we don’t really know it, and that the way to find an answer is to keep trying harder. Given Mendeleev and the mind-wanderers, is trying harder the way to find an answer?","choices":["Trying harder works. If you know something, enough effort will bring it out","Trying harder can keep you stuck. Sometimes stepping away is what makes the answer reachable","Trying harder works as long as you’ve studied enough"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Trying harder can keep you stuck. What you can do at any moment depends on what you’ve learned and on the mode your brain is in, because each mode gives you access to a different part of what you know. Trying harder keeps your brain in the focused mode. Stepping away, resting, or sleeping can shift it into a mode that reaches the answer.</p><p>Many of history’s most productive thinkers built mode shifts into their days. Charles Darwin spent a few focused hours at his desk each morning, then walked his gravel “thinking path.” Beethoven walked for hours in nature, where ideas for his compositions often came to him.</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">Your brain operates in different modes, like focused work, mind-wandering, and sleep. Each mode changes which parts of the brain can communicate. The book calls the pattern of connections in each mode its routing architecture. Changing modes changes what you can reach, without changing anything you know.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">The book devotes two chapters to routing: how the brain shifts between modes, and how to shift modes on purpose.</p>"}],"next":{"id":"capability-accessibility-model","name":"The Capability-Accessibility Model","reason":"What are the two things that determine what you can do at any moment?"},"links":[{"id":"solution-space","name":"Solution Space"},{"id":"the-impostor","name":"The Impostor"}],"game":null},"solution-space":{"name":"Solution Space","steps":[{"label":"The Access Line · The Machinery Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">Solution Space</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">What’s something you can’t do yet that you’d like to be able to do?</p>"},{"chunk":"<p>The book describes everything you can currently do as a piece of land surrounded by a fence. Everything inside the fence is your solution space: the problems you’ve already learned to solve.</p><p>Reading this sentence is inside the fence. So are walking across your bedroom, making toast, and telling a bus from a bicycle. Playing Rachmaninoff’s Third Piano Concerto is probably outside it.</p>"},{"ask":"If you wanted to bring the Rachmaninoff concerto inside your fence, what would it take?","choices":["A different brain","Years of learning to play the piano","Believing you can do it"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Years of learning to play the piano. The skills needed to play that concerto don’t exist in your brain yet, so for now it’s outside the fence, as it would be for anyone who hasn’t learned to play.</p><p>Learning is the only way to move the fence outward.</p>"},{"ask":"Your fence marks the problems you can solve today. What does it tell you about what you’re capable of learning?","choices":["The fence shows the limit of what you’ll ever be able to learn","The fence tells you nothing about what you can learn. It shows where your learning has brought you so far","The fence shows how much natural talent you have"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>It tells you nothing about that. It shows where your learning has brought you so far, not how far it can go.</p><p>The Impostor looks at that fence and sees a concrete wall, fixed at birth by your genes. That’s the Special Brain Story. The Genius built the fence through learning, and more learning moves it.</p>"},{"chunk":"<p>Moving the fence isn’t the only way to do more. In the sleep study from the Capability-Accessibility Model station, the sleepers found a hidden shortcut without being taught anything new. The shortcut was inside their fence all along. Sleep shifted their brains into a mode that could reach it.</p><p>At any given moment, your brain can reach only part of your solution space, depending on the mode it’s in.</p>"},{"ask":"Many people believe that what they can do is fixed by who they are. What do the fence and the sleep study say?","choices":["Capability is fixed. Some things are beyond certain people","Capability changes. It depends on what you’ve learned and what you can access, and both can change","Capability grows with learning, but only up to a point"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>What you’re capable of isn’t fixed. It depends on two things you can change: what you’ve learned, and what your brain can access in its current mode. Learning moves the fence outward. Shifting modes lets you reach more of the land inside it.</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">Your solution space is the set of problems you can currently solve. The book pictures it as land inside a fence. Learning moves the fence outward, and shifting your brain’s mode lets you reach more of the land inside it.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">The book describes your solution space as the land you’ve fenced off so far, and learning as moving the fence posts outward.</p>"}],"next":{"id":"the-genius","name":"The Genius","reason":"What built your solution space in the first place?"},"links":[{"id":"progressive-composition","name":"Progressive Composition"},{"id":"routing-architecture","name":"Routing Architecture"},{"id":"capability-accessibility-model","name":"The Capability-Accessibility Model"}],"game":null},"unreliable-narrator":{"name":"The Unreliable Narrator","steps":[{"label":"The Impostor Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">The Unreliable Narrator</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">If you were a judge deciding whether to grant parole, what would you base your decision on?</p>"},{"chunk":"<p>Researchers reviewed more than a thousand parole decisions made by eight judges in Israel over ten months. The chance that a prisoner would be granted parole didn’t depend much on the severity of the crime or on the prisoner’s record.</p><p>It depended on how long it had been since the judge’s last meal break. Right after a break, prisoners had about a 65 percent chance of parole. By the end of a long session without one, that chance had fallen close to zero.</p><p class=\"lf-cite\">Danziger, Levav &amp; Avnaim-Pesso, 2011</p>"},{"ask":"If you had asked those judges why they made each decision, what do you think they would have said?","choices":["That they were hungry or tired","That each decision was based on the facts of the case","That they didn’t know"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>They would have explained each decision using the facts of the case, and they would have believed it. Nothing in their explanations would have mentioned how long it had been since their last break.</p><p>Whatever was driving the pattern, whether hunger or mental fatigue, was outside their awareness, so it never appeared in their explanations.</p>"},{"ask":"In another study, people looked at one pair of photos after another and picked the face they found more attractive. Then the researcher secretly swapped the photos and asked them to explain their choice while showing them the face they had rejected. The switch was caught only about one time in four. The rest of the time, people explained in detail why they had chosen a face they hadn’t chosen, saying things like “she has kind eyes.” What does that tell you about where our explanations come from?","choices":["The people were lying to please the researcher","The part of the brain that explains our choices doesn’t know why we made them, so it builds a believable story","The people had poor eyesight"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>It tells you that the part of the brain that explains our choices doesn’t have access to the real reasons. Its job is to produce an explanation that sounds right to other people, and it does that whether or not it knows the truth.</p><p>The made-up explanations were just as detailed and heartfelt as the ones people gave for their real choices. So feeling sure of our reasons tells us very little about whether they’re true.</p><p class=\"lf-cite\">Johansson, Hall et al., 2005</p>"},{"ask":"Dennis, the patient from the Capability-Accessibility Model station, insisted he couldn’t recognize written words after his stroke. His doctor handed him cards with the names of plants and animals and asked him to sort them into two piles with his left hand, as fast as he could. He sorted all but one correctly. The chance of doing that by guessing was less than 1 in 4,000. What does that tell you?","choices":["He had been pretending he couldn’t read","Part of his brain could still recognize the words, but the part that talks didn’t know it","He got lucky"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>The right side of Dennis’s brain, which controls the left hand, was still receiving what his eyes saw, and it could read the words well enough to guide that hand. But that information never reached the part of his brain that talks. So he honestly believed he couldn’t do it, and he was as surprised by the result as everyone else in the room.</p><p>The narrator in your head can be wrong about what you’re capable of, not only about why you do things.</p>"},{"ask":"We tend to think that we know why we do what we do, and that we know what we’re capable of. What do the judges, the swapped photos, and Dennis say about that?","choices":["We know our own reasons and abilities, because we have direct access to them","The voice that explains us often doesn’t know the real reasons, or everything we can do","We know our reasons, except when the stakes are high"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>The voice that explains you often doesn’t know the real causes. It tells a story about your choices and your abilities either way. The book calls that voice the Impostor.</p><p>Its stories about what you can’t do are no more reliable than its stories about why you picked a face.</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">The part of your brain that explains your choices often doesn’t have access to the real reasons for them. It produces a believable story, and you believe it too. The same narrator also tells you what you can and can’t do, and it’s just as unreliable about that.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">This station follows the book’s chapter “An Unreliable Narrator.”</p>"}],"next":{"id":"special-brain-story","name":"The Special Brain Story","reason":"What is the most damaging story the narrator tells?"},"links":[{"id":"the-impostor","name":"The Impostor"}],"game":null},"trained-reasoning":{"name":"Trained Reasoning","steps":[{"label":"The Bias Line","chunk":"<h2 class=\"lf-station-name\">Trained Reasoning</h2><p class=\"lf-opener\">Where do you think people’s ability to reason comes from?</p>"},{"ask":"Here’s a famous logic puzzle. Four cards lie on a table, showing A, D, 4, and 7. Each card has a letter on one side and a number on the other. The rule is: if a card has a vowel on one side, it has an even number on the other side. Which cards do you need to turn over to check whether the rule is being followed?","choices":["A and 4","A and 7","A only","All four cards"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>A and 7. If the A has an odd number on the back, the rule is broken. If the 7 has a vowel on the back, the rule is broken too. The 4 doesn’t matter, because the rule says nothing about what must be on the back of an even number.</p><p>Most people choose A and 4. In study after study, fewer than one in ten people get this right.</p><p class=\"lf-cite\">The Wason selection task, 1966</p>"},{"ask":"Now try the same puzzle in a different form. You’re a bartender checking a rule: if someone is drinking beer, they must be over 21. Four people are at the bar. One is drinking beer, one is drinking soda, one is 25, and one is 16. Whose drink or age do you need to check?","choices":["The beer drinker and the 16-year-old","The beer drinker and the 25-year-old","Everyone at the bar"],"correct":0,"answer":"<p>The beer drinker and the 16-year-old. Most people get this version right almost immediately. In studies, about three out of four people solve it.</p><p>It’s the same logical structure as the card puzzle. The beer drinker is the A, and the 16-year-old is the 7.</p><p class=\"lf-cite\">Griggs &amp; Cox, 1982</p>"},{"ask":"The logic of the two puzzles is identical. So why is one so much easier than the other?","choices":["The letters and numbers in the card puzzle are confusing to look at","We’ve learned patterns for situations like checking who’s allowed to drink, but not for abstract letters and numbers","People try harder on the bar puzzle"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Because reasoning depends on patterns we’ve learned in specific situations. Most adults have plenty of experience with rules about who’s allowed to do what, so the bar puzzle matches patterns their brains have already built. Abstract letters and numbers match nothing they’ve learned, so they have to work the problem out slowly with the conscious mind, where most people get it wrong.</p>"},{"ask":"The card puzzle felt like hard, careful thinking. The bar puzzle felt easy. We usually treat slow, effortful reasoning as the highest form of thinking. Given what you’ve just seen, what is that effort a sign of?","choices":["That you’re using your intelligence at full power","That you’ve run into a problem your brain hasn’t built the patterns to solve yet","That the problem is impossible"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>It’s a sign that you’ve run into a problem your brain hasn’t built the patterns for yet. Effortful reasoning is what happens when the conscious mind has to handle a problem on its own, because no expert network is available to do it.</p><p>With practice, those patterns get built, and the same kind of problem starts to feel easy. That’s also why formal logic has to be learned step by step, like any other skill.</p>"},{"ask":"The common view is that reasoning is a general ability, and some people are logical and others aren’t. The same people found identical logic hard in one form and easy in another. Knowing that, what’s the reality?","choices":["Reasoning is a general ability. Logical people can reason well about anything","Reasoning is built from learned patterns, like every other skill, and it can be trained","Some people are born more logical, and training helps a little"],"correct":1,"answer":"<p>Reasoning is a skill, built by the same learning process as walking, reading, and everything else you do. It depends on the patterns you’ve learned, which is why the same people found identical logic easy in one form and hard in another.</p>"},{"label":"What this station shows","chunk":"<p class=\"lf-takeaway\">Reasoning feels like a general ability you can apply to anything. But the same logic problem can be easy in one form and nearly impossible in another. Reasoning depends on the patterns you’ve learned, which makes it a skill like any other, built through learning.</p><p class=\"lf-bookref\">The book argues that thinking skills like logic and math are built by the same learning process as walking and talking.</p>"}],"next":{"id":"special-brain-story","name":"The Special Brain Story","reason":"What story ties all of these mistaken beliefs together?"},"links":[{"id":"domain-specific-intelligence","name":"Domain-Specific Intelligence"},{"id":"bottleneck-chunking","name":"The Bottleneck and Chunking"},{"id":"introspective-bias","name":"Introspective Bias"}],"game":null}};

window.AtlasCards = (function () {
  var DONE_KEY = 'gi.atlas.done';
  function done() { try { return JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(DONE_KEY) || '[]'); } catch (e) { return []; } }
  function markDone(id) {
    try { var d = done(); if (d.indexOf(id) < 0) { d.push(id); localStorage.setItem(DONE_KEY, JSON.stringify(d)); } } catch (e) {}
    if (window.AtlasCards.onDone) window.AtlasCards.onDone(id);
  }
  function esc(s) { return String(s).replace(/&/g, '&amp;').replace(/</g, '&lt;').replace(/>/g, '&gt;').replace(/"/g, '&quot;'); }
  function injectStyles() {
    if (document.getElementById('atlas-cards-styles')) return;
    var el = document.createElement('style'); el.id = 'atlas-cards-styles';
    el.textContent =
      '.lf-station-name{font-family:var(--serif,Georgia,serif);font-size:clamp(26px,5vw,34px);line-height:1.2;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:-.01em;margin:0 0 16px;}' +
      '.lf-opener{font-size:clamp(19px,3.6vw,22px);line-height:1.45;margin:0;}' +
      '.lf-takeaway{font-size:clamp(18px,3.4vw,21px);line-height:1.5;}' +
      '.lf-bookref{font-size:15px;font-style:italic;color:var(--muted,#666);line-height:1.55;margin-top:14px;}' +
      '.ac-next{display:block;text-decoration:none;border:1px solid var(--rule,#e0dcd4);border-left:3px solid var(--red,#c44b3a);border-radius:4px;padding:16px 18px;margin:0 0 18px;background:#fff;}' +
      '.ac-next:hover{background:#fdfbf7;}' +
      '.ac-kicker{font-family:var(--mono,"Courier New",monospace);font-size:10px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.2em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted,#666);margin:0 0 8px;}' +
      '.ac-next-name{font-size:21px;font-weight:600;color:var(--red,#c44b3a);margin:0 0 4px;}' +
      '.ac-next-reason{font-size:16px;color:var(--muted,#666);line-height:1.5;margin:0;}' +
      '.ac-links{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;margin:0 0 18px;}' +
      '.ac-link{font-size:15px;text-decoration:none;border:1px solid var(--rule,#e0dcd4);border-radius:3px;padding:7px 12px;background:#fff;}' +
      '.ac-link:hover{border-color:var(--red,#c44b3a);color:var(--red,#c44b3a);}' +
      '.ac-map{font-family:var(--mono,"Courier New",monospace);font-size:12px;letter-spacing:.08em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted,#666);text-decoration:none;}' +
      '.ac-map:hover{color:var(--red,#c44b3a);}';
    document.head.appendChild(el);
  }
  function stationHref(base, id) { return base + 'index.html#' + id; }
  function nextCard(id, base) {
    var c = window.ATLAS_CARDS[id], h = '';
    if (c.next) {
      h += '<a class="ac-next" data-station="' + c.next.id + '" href="' + stationHref(base, c.next.id) + '">' +
        '<p class="ac-kicker">Next station</p><p class="ac-next-name">' + esc(c.next.name) + ' &rarr;</p>' +
        '<p class="ac-next-reason">' + esc(c.next.reason) + '</p></a>';
    }
    if (c.links.length) {
      h += '<p class="ac-kicker">Connected stations</p><div class="ac-links">' +
        c.links.map(function (l) { return '<a class="ac-link" data-station="' + l.id + '" href="' + stationHref(base, l.id) + '">' + esc(l.name) + '</a>'; }).join('') + '</div>';
    }
    var slug = c.game ? c.game.url.split('/').pop().split('.')[0] : null;
    if (c.game && window.launched && window.launched(slug)) {
      h += '<p class="ac-kicker">A game that shows this idea</p><div class="ac-links"><a class="ac-link" href="' + base + c.game.url + '">Play ' + esc(c.game.name) + '</a></div>';
    }
    h += '<a class="ac-map" href="' + base + 'index.html">See the whole Map &rarr;</a>';
    return { label: 'Where to next', chunk: h };
  }
  var current = null;
  /* opts.base: path to the atlas folder. opts.inPlace: the page is the Map
     itself, so station links open the next station's cards here instead of
     loading a page. */
  function open(id, opts) {
    opts = opts || {};
    var c = window.ATLAS_CARDS[id]; if (!c || !window.LearnFlow) return;
    injectStyles();
    if (current) { var prev = current; current = null; prev.close(); }
    var base = opts.base || '';
    /* card media paths are written relative to the atlas folder */
    var steps = c.steps.map(function (s) {
      if (!base) return s;
      var f = function (h) { return h && h.replace(/src="media\//g, 'src="' + base + 'media/'); };
      return Object.assign({}, s, { chunk: f(s.chunk), answer: f(s.answer) });
    }).concat([nextCard(id, base)]);
    var handle = LearnFlow.open(steps, {
      doneLabel: 'Done',
      onComplete: function () { markDone(id); },
      onClose: function () { if (current === handle) current = null; if (opts.onClose) opts.onClose(); }
    });
    current = handle;
    var modals = document.querySelectorAll('.lf-modal'), modal = modals[modals.length - 1];
    if (modal) modal.addEventListener('click', function (e) {
      var a = e.target.closest && e.target.closest('a');
      if (!a) return;
      markDone(id);
      var to = a.getAttribute('data-station');
      if (to && opts.inPlace) { e.preventDefault(); open(to, opts); if (history.replaceState) history.replaceState(null, '', '#' + to); }
    });
    return handle;
  }
  return { open: open, done: done, markDone: markDone };
})();
